---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import UtilityAssistantNav from '../../../components/UtilityAssistantNav.astro';
import { apps } from '../../../data/site';

const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'utility-assistant');
const appName = app?.name ?? 'Utility Assistant';
const effectiveDate = 'August 30, 2026';
const feedbackEmail = 'feedback@smalltechstack.com';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${appName} Privacy Policy`}
  description={`Privacy policy for ${appName}.`}
  image={false}
>
  <UtilityAssistantNav current="privacy" />
  <article class="policy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Effective {effectiveDate}</p>
    <h1>{appName} Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>
      {appName} is provided by Small Tech Stack LLC. This policy explains what
      information the app handles, how it is used, and what choices you have.
    </p>

    <h2>Information You Enter</h2>
    <p>{appName} stores information you choose to add to the app, including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Service locations, such as a nickname and ZIP code.</li>
      <li>Utility providers and account nicknames.</li>
      <li>
        Utility bills, billing periods, statement dates, due dates, totals,
        notes, and bill line items.
      </li>
      <li>
        Meter readings, including reading dates, values, units, and whether a
        reading was entered manually or came from a bill.
      </li>
      <li>
        Timeline events you create, such as home, occupancy, appliance, rate, or
        other utility-related events.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This information is used to organize your utility history, calculate usage
      and cost trends, show charts, and generate insights about bills, weather,
      meter readings, and recurring charges.
    </p>

    <h2>Weather Data</h2>
    <p>
      If you enter a ZIP code, {appName} may use it to look up weather data for
      that service location. The app sends the ZIP code to Open-Meteo's
      geocoding service to estimate latitude, longitude, and time zone, and then
      sends those coordinates and date ranges to Open-Meteo to retrieve
      historical weather information.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weather data is used to calculate temperature, heating degree days, and
      cooling degree days so the app can help explain utility usage and cost
      changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open-Meteo is a third-party weather service. Small Tech Stack LLC does not
      control Open-Meteo's privacy practices.
    </p>

    <h2>iCloud Sync</h2>
    <p>
      {appName} uses Apple's private CloudKit database to sync app data between
      your devices when iCloud is available for your Apple Account. This sync is
      provided by Apple through your iCloud account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Small Tech Stack LLC does not operate a separate account system for
      {appName} and does not provide its own server for storing your utility
      bills.
    </p>

    <h2>Feedback Emails</h2>
    <p>
      The Help screen includes links to request features or report bugs by
      email. If you choose to send feedback, the email is addressed to
      <a href={`mailto:${feedbackEmail}`}>{feedbackEmail}</a> and may include
      app diagnostic context such as:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>App name.</li>
      <li>App version and build number.</li>
      <li>iOS version.</li>
      <li>Device type and hardware identifier.</li>
      <li>Locale.</li>
      <li>Time the email was created.</li>
      <li>Anything you write in the email.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Email is optional. Do not include utility account numbers, passwords,
      payment information, or other sensitive details unless you intentionally
      choose to share them.
    </p>

    <h2>Data We Do Not Use</h2>
    <p>
      {appName} does not use your data for third-party advertising. {appName}
      does not sell your personal information. {appName} does not use your data
      to track you across apps or websites owned by other companies.
    </p>

    <h2>Data Sharing</h2>
    <p>
      {appName} may share limited information only as needed to provide app
      functionality:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Apple iCloud/CloudKit may store and sync the app data associated with
        your Apple Account.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open-Meteo receives ZIP code, coordinate, date range, and weather
        request information when weather data is retrieved.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your email provider and Small Tech Stack LLC receive the contents of any
        feedback email you choose to send.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data Storage and Retention</h2>
    <p>
      {appName} stores app data on your device and, when iCloud sync is
      available, in your private iCloud database. Data remains there until you
      edit it, delete it, replace it through an import, remove the app and its
      data, or manage it through Apple's iCloud settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app also lets you export and import your data. Export files are
      controlled by you and may contain your utility history, locations,
      providers, bills, timeline events, meter readings, and weather data.
    </p>

    <h2>Your Choices</h2>
    <p>You can:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Avoid entering a ZIP code if you do not want weather lookup for a location.</li>
      <li>Edit or delete app data in the app.</li>
      <li>Export your app data.</li>
      <li>Replace app data by importing a backup file.</li>
      <li>Manage iCloud availability through your Apple Account and device settings.</li>
      <li>Contact Small Tech Stack LLC about privacy questions or data requests.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      {appName} is not directed to children. The app is intended for people
      managing household or property utility information.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      Small Tech Stack LLC may update this policy as {appName} changes. If the
      app's data practices materially change, this policy should be updated
      before or at the time those changes are released.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Small Tech Stack LLC<br />
      Email: <a href={`mailto:${feedbackEmail}`}>{feedbackEmail}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
